# Convex as the implementation reference

HaiFHIR copies Convex's **database engine**, not Convex the BaaS. For any implementation (persistence, transactions, commit, indexes, subscriptions, sync), look up how [get-convex/convex-backend](https://github.com/get-convex/convex-backend) does it, then adapt to our constraints.

Before writing code, name the Convex source (crate path + type/function, e.g. `crates/database/src/committer.rs` `validate_commit`). Match that shape unless architecture.md forbids it. Do not invent a parallel design (SQL table per resource, long-lived SQL transactions around handlers, skip read sets).

## Do it the Convex way

- Document log + index log behind a small `Persistence` trait (`crates/common/src/persistence/mod.rs`, `crates/sqlite/src/lib.rs`).
- In-memory `Transaction` with snapshot timestamp, `ReadSet`, buffered writes (`crates/database/src/transaction.rs`).
- One committer: OCC on read-set overlap, then persist, then publish (`crates/database/src/committer.rs`).
- Write log + subscriptions for invalidation (`write_log.rs`, `subscription.rs`).
- Sync: subscribe / mutate / transition (`crates/sync/src/worker.rs`), with FHIR operation names instead of UDF paths.

## Tailor to HaiFHIR

We are not hosting other people's SaaS. No user-defined tables, schemas, or functions.

- Schema = FHIR resource types we ship. Functions = FHIR operations we ship (Rust handlers on `Transaction`, not V8).
- Clients only call those operations. Adding a type or operation is a HaiFHIR change.
- Do not port `crates/isolate`, `udf-runtime`, CLI `push_config`, Funrun, customer `schema.ts`, or one SQL table named `Patient`.

Details: `docs/architecture.md`.
